##! Please refer to the documentation at
##! https://coreruleset.org/docs/development/regex_assembly/.

##! Bash - Tilde Expansion
##! Detects the following patterns which are common in Unix shell scripts and one-liners

##! Bash only expands a tilde-prefix at the start of a word, so require
##! start-of-string or a character that can precede one. This drops FPs on
##! values like "cookie=abc~+1", which bash leaves alone, while keeping
##! shell-chained commands like "cmd;~+1", "cmd|~+1" and "$(~+1)".
##!
##! '=' and ':' are included because bash also expands tilde-prefixes in
##! words that look like variable assignments, even as plain command
##! arguments: `echo x=~+1` and `echo c=/tmp:~+1` both expand.

##!^ (?:^|[\s;|&`$(<>=:])

~\+$
~\+\d+
~-$
~-\d+
